room      lemma_3
kind      geometry
import    Newton, Principia, Andrew Motte trans., 1729 (Wikisource); Book I, Section I, Lemma III; Plate 1, Fig. 6.
caption   The same ultimate equality of inscribed and circumscribed figures holds even when the breadths of the parallelograms are unequal.
final     2

station 1
  ceiling   eq0 :: \text{circ}-\text{insc} \;=\; \textstyle\sum (\text{unequal breadths})\;\longrightarrow\;0
  gloss   The curve aE over the baseline AE, with stepped rectangles of unequal breadths AB, BC, CD standing under the curve.
  color   stepblue  #1E6FE0
  color   basegreen #00A35A
  panel
    point   A  @(0,0)   marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point   B  @(3,0)   marker=dot label=$B$ at=S
    point   C  @(4.6,0) marker=dot label=$C$ at=S
    point   D  @(6.6,0) marker=dot label=$D$ at=S
    point   E  @(8,0)   marker=dot label=$E$ at=SE
    point   ptA @(0,1.4)  color=stepblue label=$a$ at=NW
    point   ptb @(3,2.9)  color=stepblue label=$b$ at=N
    point   ptc @(4.6,3.4) color=stepblue label=$c$ at=N
    point   ptd @(6.6,3.9) color=stepblue label=$d$ at=N
    point   ptE @(8,4.2)  color=stepblue label=$E$ at=NE
    point   A1 @(0,1.4)   color=stepblue
    point   B1 @(3,1.4)   color=stepblue
    point   C1 @(4.6,2.9) color=stepblue
    point   D1 @(6.6,3.4) color=stepblue
    polyline curve ptA ptb ptc ptd ptE   color=stepblue
    segment baseAE A E   color=basegreen
    polygon rect1 A B B1 A1   color=stepblue heart
    polygon rect2 B C C1 B1   color=stepblue
    polygon rect3 C D D1 C1   color=stepblue
  text
    In the same figure as before (Pl.~1, Fig.~6), \{stepblue|the curve $acE$} stands above \{basegreen|the base $AE$}, but now we take \emph{unequal} bases $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $\&c.$, the first wide and those following narrower. Upon them we erect \{stepblue|the stepped parallelograms} under the curve, just as in \textbf{Lemma~II}, except that here their breadths need not agree. The earlier lemma proved the ultimate equality of inscribed and circumscribed figures only for equal bases; this lemma asserts that the same conclusion survives when the breadths differ. We must therefore show that the total excess of the circumscribed figure over the inscribed one still vanishes as every base shrinks. The whole of that excess will be trapped within a single rectangle built on the \emph{widest} base.

station 2
  ceiling   eq1 :: FAaf \;>\; (\text{circ}-\text{insc}), \quad AF\to0 \;\Rightarrow\; FAaf\to0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{insc}=\text{circ}
  gloss   The bounding parallelogram FAaf on the greatest breadth AF contains the entire error between inscribed and circumscribed figures; as AF shrinks the parallelogram, and hence the error, becomes less than any given rectangle.
  color   boundred    #D81B60
  color   widthorange #E8770A
  color   stepblue    #1E6FE0
  color   basegreen   #00A35A
  panel
    point   A  @(0,0)   marker=dot label=$A$ at=SW
    point   F  @(3,0)   marker=dot label=$F$ at=S
    point   E  @(8,0)   marker=dot
    point   ptA @(0,1.4) color=stepblue label=$a$ at=NW
    point   fpt @(3,1.4) color=stepblue label=$f$ at=NE
    polyline curve2 ptA fpt   color=stepblue
    segment baseAE A E   color=basegreen
    segment AF A F   color=widthorange
    polygon FAaf A F fpt ptA   color=boundred heart
  text
    Let \{widthorange|$AF$} be taken equal to the greatest of the breadths, and complete \{boundred|the parallelogram $FAaf$} upon it. Because this single rectangle is at least as tall and as wide as any of the differences between the circumscribed and inscribed steps, \{boundred|$FAaf$} is greater than the whole excess of the circumscribed figure over the inscribed one. But as the bases are all diminished \emph{in infinitum}, \{widthorange|the greatest breadth $AF$} likewise shrinks toward nothing, so \{boundred|$FAaf$} becomes less than any given rectangle. Hence the trapped error, lying wholly within \{boundred|$FAaf$}, also vanishes, and \{stepblue|the inscribed} and circumscribed figures along \{basegreen|the base $AE$} become ultimately equal exactly as in \textbf{Lemma~II}. \textit{Q.E.D.}
